  "_meta": {
    "accent": "southern",
    "hints": "Founder-editable drafts: English-speaker approximations; regional voice differences remain in the audio. Hyphens separate Vietnamese syllables: give each chunk one beat, without English stress. Initial d/gi/v use y; đ uses d; r stays r; tr stays tr (no strongly separate r); Vietnamese th uses plain t, like tea, never English th. Eh (also e in closed hints such as em/dep/sem) is bet; ay for ê is the first part of say, held steady without an ee offglide; lên/mệt use the southern central-vowel cues luhn/muht. Ee is the vowel in see; gì uses yee. Uu uses an oo-like tongue position with unrounded lips; uh is a relaxed mid vowel without English r; uuh holds the unrounded vowel longer. Ow always rhymes with cow, including sow/bow. Ah is father, kept short for ă and where specified; aw is rounded law (use the audio if cot/caught sound alike); oh is a steady rounded o. Oy is a broad cue for oi/ôi, not an assertion that they are identical. Uhy glides from uh toward ee; eeoo glides from ee toward oo; booy/yooy glide from oo toward ee; duhow glides from short uh toward rounded oo; teow glides from eh toward oo: all within one beat. Ng is the end of sing, even initially, without an added g or preliminary uh; ny is the middle of canyon compressed into the onset; kh is back-of-mouth friction (or the selected voice’s breathy variant), not plain k. Final p/t/k close without audible release or an added vowel; final ng may include lip closure after rounded vowels. Every non-level syllable has a tone bracket: none means ngang (level); [rising] is sắc; [falling] is huyền; [dipping] is the shared hỏi/ngã dip then rise; [low] is nặng’s low register, which may recover slightly on open/nasal-final syllables, without a compulsory glottal choke; [low, clipped] adds final-stop closure. These are broad directions, not fixed pitch tracks.",
    "sound_reference": "General notation background only (not the target accent): James P. Kirby (2011), Vietnamese (Hanoi Vietnamese), https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100311000181; southern vowel and consonant comparison: Andrea Hoa Pham (2009), https://people.clas.ufl.edu/apham/files/Pham-2009-The-identity-of-non-identified-sounds-glottal-stop-prevocalic-w-and-tripthongs-in-Vietnamese.pdf"
  }
